The old brass telescope rested on a heavy oak tripod by the attic window, pointed out toward the distant, hazy line where the hills met the sky. Dust motes drifted lazily in the slanting shafts of afternoon light that cut through the cracked panes, illuminating a small ecosystem of forgotten boxes and yellowed paperbacks. For years, the lens had been out of alignment, showing only a blurred smear of green and blue whenever anyone dared to peer through it. But after a careful tightening of the outer ring with a jeweler's screwdriver, the focus snapped into sharp, startling clarity. Suddenly, the chimney pots on the far ridge, the solitary hawk circling the valley, and the peeling paint on the distant water tower were brought close enough to touch. It reminded him that most things in life do not need to be replaced when they become unclear; they often just require a subtle adjustment to bring the hidden details back into view.
